1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
They're super, they're crazy,

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:06,000
and they can be found in everything.

3
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:11,040
From carpet to computers, books to boats,

4
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:13,740
automobiles to airplanes.

5
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:16,760
They're essential to mail bonding, reef repair,

6
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:18,660
and cut control.

7
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:23,060
Without them, our material goods would simply fall apart.

8
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:27,660
Now, the stuck-up tacky world of glue on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,640
Concealed within our machines and structures is a substance

10
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:46,220
that is essential to their functioning.

11
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:49,960
This hidden ally makes them stronger, cheaper, lighter,

12
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:52,460
even sexier.

13
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:56,960
And it's the least sexy substance of them all, glue.

14
00:00:57,060 --> 00:00:59,460
Like Superman's alter ego, Clark Kent,

15
00:00:59,461 --> 00:01:01,460
goes about its duty unnoticed.

16
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:06,700
But when called upon, it can exhibit superhuman strength.

17
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:11,600
A properly used adhesive is not apparent to a person using

18
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:12,700
whatever it is.

19
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,200
You don't see it.

20
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:15,300
So you don't know it's there.

21
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:18,680
And therefore, you don't have a reason to appreciate it.

22
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:20,520
If we know anything about glue,

23
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:24,220
we know that it needs to be strong.

24
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:26,860
To test this, we're going to stack the odds against an

25
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:27,920
adhesive.

26
00:01:27,921 --> 00:01:30,260
Mr. Sticky's underwater glue.

27
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,820
Not only will the glue have to hoist this truck up off the

28
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:33,760
ground.

29
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:37,200
On top of that, the lifting device that we're going to use,

30
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:40,660
which we have right here, is going to be bonded completely

31
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:41,460
underwater.

32
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:46,100
And we're also going to be applying the glue underwater.

33
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,580
This underwater glue is an epoxy,

34
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:51,780
a category of glues that have a catalyst and a resin, which

35
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:56,920
combine during application to chemically react and cure.

36
00:01:56,921 --> 00:02:00,520
The adhesive is designed to be applied to wet or dry surfaces,

37
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:04,320
or, as we have chosen, to something totally submerged

38
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:05,920
in water.

39
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:08,460
The way that Mr. Stickey's works underwater

40
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:11,060
is that it has the same polarity as water.

41
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:15,380
As you know, two north magnets will push each other or repel,

42
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:17,680
and the south and north magnet will attract.

43
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:20,440
So what we believe that's happening on a molecular level

44
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:23,220
is that as Mr. Stickey's adhesive

45
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:25,920
is being pushed through the water near the joint,

46
00:02:25,921 --> 00:02:27,920
it's actually pushing the water out of the way,

47
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:30,020
much like two north magnets.

48
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:32,220
And then when it gets to the substrate,

49
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:36,260
it's a north and a south, and it sticks.

50
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:39,760
But wait, who needs an underwater glue anyway?

51
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:42,880
While there are a number of direct underwater applications,

52
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:45,580
you know, repairing your pool, putting tiles on underwater,

53
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:49,480
fixing main drain covers, fixing gutters,

54
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:51,520
we've actually had some great successes

55
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:55,480
in terms of bonding coral and repairing coral reefs.

56
00:02:55,481 --> 00:02:57,780
The reality is that many substrates have water

57
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:01,180
as part of them.

58
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:03,280
Does this underwater glue have the strength

59
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:04,720
to do the trick?

60
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:05,920
Stick around.

61
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:07,620
We'll see what a little glue can do.

62
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:20,540
While glue's strength may be its most dramatic characteristic,

63
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:23,480
its omnipresence is equally impressive.

64
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:24,840
From the time you get up in the morning

65
00:03:24,841 --> 00:03:28,080
to the time you go to bed at night, you must touch things

66
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:30,980
that have adhesives in them probably every five or ten

67
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:32,080
minutes.

68
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:34,880
I mean, there really isn't an industry that adhesives don't

69
00:03:34,980 --> 00:03:39,080
play a part, whether it's construction, medical devices,

70
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:42,900
surgical adhesives, automotive, electronics, transportation,

71
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
book binding, packaged goods.

72
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:47,200
It's really everywhere.

73
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:50,840
But what would life be like without adhesives?

74
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:54,100
Imagine for a moment the room that you're sitting in without

75
00:03:54,101 --> 00:03:58,100
virtually nothing around you could stay together.

76
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:02,100
Furniture, wallpaper, carpeting, even the plywood in the walls

77
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:06,180
would come apart. TV, VCR, cell phone, DVDs

78
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:09,620
would be reduced to a pile of parts.

79
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:12,660
Shoes, envelopes, pens, useless.

80
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:14,160
Crowns would fall off teeth.

81
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:18,520
And watch out for that baby, his diaper's about to disintegrate.

82
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:22,600
Glue is everywhere, and it's more than a $20 billion a year

83
00:04:22,601 --> 00:04:24,400
a year global business.

84
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:26,500
The industry sells adhesives manufactured

85
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:28,100
from animal or plant derivatives

86
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,840
or synthesized from petroleum.

87
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:33,580
Whether natural or synthetic, glues are made up

88
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:36,620
of polymers characterized by their high molecular weight,

89
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:39,360
which give them their strength.

90
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:41,280
If you start with a chemical unit,

91
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:44,780
a single one chemical unit, which we refer to as monomer,

92
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:46,420
mono meaning one, right?

93
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:48,100
We have one unit.

94
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:50,960
If we take a bunch of these one units

95
00:04:50,961 --> 00:04:56,500
and hook them together, you create what is called a polymer.

96
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:57,880
You have one stick.

97
00:04:58,000 --> 00:04:58,700
I give it to you.

98
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:00,220
You go, you can break it.

99
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:02,560
I give you a bunch of those same sticks together.

100
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:04,040
They don't break.

101
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:08,680
The reason why they don't break is because together they have

102
00:05:08,681 --> 00:05:11,220
formed a tougher, more resilient animal.

103
00:05:12,860 --> 00:05:16,740
Glues stick in two ways, mechanical and chemical bonds.

104
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:20,100
One is a mechanical interlocking

105
00:05:20,101 --> 00:05:24,000
in which molecules get tangled up with roughness in the surface,

106
00:05:24,001 --> 00:05:26,500
sort of in the same way that when you bring two pieces of Velcro

107
00:05:26,501 --> 00:05:28,800
together, one is hooks and one is loops,

108
00:05:28,801 --> 00:05:30,540
and they get tangled up.

109
00:05:30,541 --> 00:05:32,740
The other is through chemical interactions.

110
00:05:32,741 --> 00:05:34,940
They can be very strong covalent bonds,

111
00:05:34,941 --> 00:05:37,760
in which there are two atoms linked together

112
00:05:37,761 --> 00:05:40,660
to form a very strong bond that's very difficult to break.

113
00:05:40,661 --> 00:05:44,860
Glues have been holding their own for thousands of years.

114
00:05:44,861 --> 00:05:48,200
The earliest were natural, derived either from plant sources

115
00:05:48,201 --> 00:05:52,500
sources or from animal protein. Hyde glues, which get their

116
00:05:52,501 --> 00:05:55,800
adhesive properties from collagen, the main protein found in animal

117
00:05:55,801 --> 00:05:59,240
skins, were probably discovered when ancient peoples found they

118
00:05:59,241 --> 00:06:03,440
could bond tools, weapons, and pottery using the sticky protein

119
00:06:03,441 --> 00:06:08,260
residue found at the bottom of their cook pots.

120
00:06:08,261 --> 00:06:11,460
The collagen, which is rendered during cooking, forms a strong

121
00:06:11,461 --> 00:06:15,560
bond by soaking into porous surfaces like wood and hardening

122
00:06:15,561 --> 00:06:20,160
cooling and drying. A casket removed from King Tut's

123
00:06:20,161 --> 00:06:25,360
tomb was sealed with animal glue. The word glue traditionally

124
00:06:25,361 --> 00:06:28,960
referred only to these hide glues, but the modern term for

125
00:06:28,961 --> 00:06:33,560
glue is adhesive. Both glue and adhesive refer to a

126
00:06:33,561 --> 00:06:36,000
substance used to stick two surfaces together.

127
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,400
Other early glues were made from tree sap.

128
00:06:41,401 --> 00:06:44,800
Sticky resins were collected from trees, applied as is,

129
00:06:44,801 --> 00:06:48,300
and allowed to dry to form a bond.

130
00:06:48,301 --> 00:06:51,300
The Romans were one of the first peoples to glue together and

131
00:06:51,301 --> 00:06:54,340
waterproof their ships with adhesives made from a blend of

132
00:06:54,341 --> 00:06:58,760
tar and beeswax. This effective mixture combined

133
00:06:58,761 --> 00:07:01,860
the stickiness of the tar with the waterproof qualities of the

134
00:07:01,861 --> 00:07:06,000
wax. By the 19th century, there was

135
00:07:06,001 --> 00:07:09,800
widespread use of starch glues to bond paper, and particularly

136
00:07:09,801 --> 00:07:12,340
and particularly wallpaper.

137
00:07:12,341 --> 00:07:15,240
Mixed with water, starches derived from wheat, rice,

138
00:07:15,241 --> 00:07:18,920
and corn develop into a highly viscous sticky paste, which

139
00:07:18,921 --> 00:07:22,160
soaks into porous papers or cardboards and bonds when

140
00:07:22,161 --> 00:07:24,900
dried.

141
00:07:24,901 --> 00:07:26,980
By the 20th century, animal glues

142
00:07:26,981 --> 00:07:29,520
were being mass produced from rendered cattle hides,

143
00:07:29,521 --> 00:07:33,580
a byproduct of the beef industry.

144
00:07:33,581 --> 00:07:35,180
During the manufacturing process,

145
00:07:35,181 --> 00:07:37,300
the hides are washed, soaked, shredded,

146
00:07:37,301 --> 00:07:39,540
to extract the gelatinous collagen.

147
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:42,680
This material dries into sheets and is then ground

148
00:07:42,780 --> 00:07:46,180
into a powder to which water is added for use.

149
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:49,040
These are protein glues extracted from hide.

150
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:50,340
And they're still very important.

151
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:52,520
The classic furniture restorers, for example,

152
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:55,580
still swear by hide glues because they're the only thing

153
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:58,820
that will bond to the old glues.

154
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:02,320
Today, three US producers of animal glue remain,

155
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:05,920
including Milligan and Higgins.

156
00:08:05,921 --> 00:08:08,820
Because of its flexibility, the product has become a specialty

157
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:13,720
glue used on guitars, organs, and veneers.

158
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:17,760
By the 1930s, the development of synthetic adhesives, which

159
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:20,860
had quality and consistency advantages over natural glues,

160
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:22,680
revolutionized the industry.

161
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:28,780
These new adhesives were offshoots of the first synthetic

162
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:31,280
rubbers and plastics.

163
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:34,140
All the major developments have really come about in the last

164
00:08:34,141 --> 00:08:37,440
in the last 100 years, which really coincides

165
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:39,540
with the development of materials

166
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:42,740
from petroleum feedstocks.

167
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:46,080
The growth of one company, National Starch and Chemical,

168
00:08:46,180 --> 00:08:47,680
mirrored the changes in the industry.

169
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:53,900
Founded in 1895 by Alexander Alexander,

170
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:55,720
the company's product line was dominated

171
00:08:55,820 --> 00:08:59,200
by starch-based adhesives.

172
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:02,060
The predominant adhesive in those days

173
00:09:02,061 --> 00:09:06,560
was the starch and dextrin-based adhesives.

174
00:09:06,660 --> 00:09:10,060
And they were used for all of these applications,

175
00:09:10,160 --> 00:09:13,700
making envelopes, making bags, making tubes,

176
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:16,400
making cups, sealing cases.

177
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:18,980
Over the years, it began to change

178
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:22,640
from those natural-based materials to synthetic.

179
00:09:22,740 --> 00:09:24,880
When you use natural-based products

180
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:29,320
based upon starch or dextrin, it would take maybe 50, 60 seconds

181
00:09:29,420 --> 00:09:31,460
to develop a tearing bond.

182
00:09:31,461 --> 00:09:34,600
Then synthetic products, polyvinyl acetate came along,

183
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:37,140
and maybe you could get a setting bond in 10 seconds.

184
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:42,080
By the 1950s, the company had developed a proprietary method

185
00:09:42,081 --> 00:09:46,120
of producing vinyl acetate, a latex that resulted from plastics

186
00:09:46,121 --> 00:09:49,420
research and was on its way to diversifying its adhesive

187
00:09:49,421 --> 00:09:50,121
portfolio.

188
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:55,020
From its humble beginning selling wallpaper paste,

189
00:09:55,560 --> 00:09:58,860
National Starch is now a worldwide $3 billion a year

190
00:09:58,861 --> 00:10:02,060
global adhesives company that sticks the labels on food

191
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:07,940
packaging and holds cars and computers together.

192
00:10:08,040 --> 00:10:10,640
The starches and the gelatins and the animal glues

193
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:13,400
had good properties when you were sticking things that

194
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:18,480
were natural, like wooden paper, but did not fit in the world

195
00:10:18,580 --> 00:10:22,380
where you now wanted to stick first metal and steel and iron

196
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:26,420
and now a wide variety of sophisticated plastics

197
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:28,860
and composites.

198
00:10:28,861 --> 00:10:32,600
In 1947, as synthetic glues were coming into vogue,

199
00:10:33,140 --> 00:10:35,860
Borden's Elmer's division introduced Elmer's Glue All,

200
00:10:36,260 --> 00:10:38,680
a synthetic glue that featured that familiar logo.

201
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:43,120
Where Elsie was the food side of the business,

202
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:47,420
Elmer represented sort of non-foods, and it took off.

203
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:51,460
It was wildly successful, and in 1967,

204
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:54,040
we actually launched Elmer into the school market.

205
00:10:54,180 --> 00:10:55,880
That's when we brought out the Elmer's school glue.

206
00:10:55,881 --> 00:11:01,960
Elmer's had another marketing coup in the 1970s with crazy glue,

207
00:11:02,620 --> 00:11:05,720
an instant glue that forms bonds on just about anything.

208
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:09,480
A plastic knob or rubber boot, model planes and model trains.

209
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:11,840
And unfortunately, fingers.

210
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:14,600
If you bonded your fingers together,

211
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,560
I'll give you a little clue as to how you get your fingers apart.

212
00:11:19,020 --> 00:11:21,720
Don't try to rip them apart like this,

213
00:11:21,721 --> 00:11:23,080
because you will rip the skin.

214
00:11:23,081 --> 00:11:27,000
What you do is take a pencil, or you can take your finger even,

215
00:11:27,380 --> 00:11:33,500
and roll it so that you start to peel the adhesive off of your fingers.

216
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:36,700
Some of you may recall the advertising in the early 70s

217
00:11:36,701 --> 00:11:39,080
that had a man with one drop of glue

218
00:11:39,081 --> 00:11:42,040
and then was hung from a hard hat and a crane.

219
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:46,400
Crazy glue strong enough to hold this man suspended in midair.

220
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:50,020
Well, that image, even today, more than 30 years later,

221
00:11:50,021 --> 00:11:52,660
is still recognizable and is remembered.

222
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:58,080
And that image inspired Art Quinn, CEO of Advanced Adhesion,

223
00:11:58,180 --> 00:12:02,080
to attempt an act of brazen one-upmanship for modern marvels.

224
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:10,260
What we have here are two pipes that we've prepared,

225
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:14,660
and we've machined the end of the pipe in such a way that there

226
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:17,900
is a small gap between the inner and the outer pipe, about

227
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,840
5,000ths of an inch all the way around.

228
00:12:20,841 --> 00:12:24,740
So that makes it possible for the glue to fill that void.

229
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:26,640
It is not a press fit.

230
00:12:26,740 --> 00:12:29,140
The glue is doing all the work.

231
00:12:29,240 --> 00:12:32,920
Art took his glue, Mr. Sticky's underwater glue,

232
00:12:33,020 --> 00:12:34,520
and applied it to the steel fitting

233
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:36,860
while it was immersed in water.

234
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:38,660
After letting it cure underwater,

235
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:40,600
Art's going to use that glue bond

236
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:44,100
to try to lift a three-ton truck.

237
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:47,640
First, the guys have to secure 6,000 pounds of pickup.

238
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:54,840
It's time for the lift.

239
00:12:54,940 --> 00:12:58,440
Let's see if Mr. Sticky's can live up to its name,

240
00:12:58,540 --> 00:13:03,380
or if three tons of truck are going to tumble to the turn.

241
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:05,880
The wires are tight.

242
00:13:05,980 --> 00:13:08,800
We've got lift off.

243
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:12,100
Every ounce of weight is pulling down on the adhesive,

244
00:13:12,200 --> 00:13:14,500
but it's going to hold.

245
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:15,600
I'm a happy guy.

246
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:16,940
It's done the job.

247
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:19,240
Fantastic.

248
00:13:19,241 --> 00:13:22,240
Don't try the stunt at home, but if you're gluing something

249
00:13:22,340 --> 00:13:25,240
a little more down to work, we've got construction glues

250
00:13:25,340 --> 00:13:30,180
that would sooner tear apart steel and give up their grip.

251
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:33,480
Worldwide, more than 47 million grade schoolers

252
00:13:33,580 --> 00:13:36,000
use Elmer's glue each week.

253
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:38,500
Glue will return on Modern Marvels.

254
00:13:44,500 --> 00:13:47,140
We now return to Glue on Modern Marvels.

255
00:13:49,240 --> 00:13:53,280
Throughout the world, the manufacture of plywood is the

256
00:13:53,380 --> 00:13:57,240
single largest application of adhesive.

