00:13:57,340 --> 00:13:59,880
It's one of many construction components that are assembled

258
00:13:59,980 --> 00:14:03,880
with adhesives before they ever reach the construction site.

259
00:14:03,980 --> 00:14:07,160
Conventional wood has a grain to it,

260
00:14:07,260 --> 00:14:10,600
so it tends to be strong in one direction and weak in the other.

261
00:14:10,700 --> 00:14:14,840
If you laminate them together in commonly three or five plies,

262
00:14:14,841 --> 00:14:19,380
together in commonly three or five plies, as it's called,

263
00:14:19,381 --> 00:14:21,040
you actually get a material that's stronger

264
00:14:21,041 --> 00:14:23,720
than the original wood.

265
00:14:23,721 --> 00:14:25,180
Increased strength is accomplished

266
00:14:25,181 --> 00:14:27,820
by laminating the layers at right angles

267
00:14:27,821 --> 00:14:30,080
so that the wood grain runs in many directions

268
00:14:30,081 --> 00:14:32,220
in the plywood.

269
00:14:32,221 --> 00:14:35,300
The plies are then bonded with a phenol formaldehyde resin.

270
00:14:35,301 --> 00:14:37,160
Adhesives are applied to the wood,

271
00:14:37,161 --> 00:14:40,240
and then the wood panels are pressed under high pressures

272
00:14:40,241 --> 00:14:42,460
at high temperatures to actually cure the adhesive.

273
00:14:44,840 --> 00:14:47,680
Phenol formaldehyde is a combination of phenol, a

274
00:14:47,681 --> 00:14:51,480
derivative of coal tar, and the preservative formaldehyde.

275
00:14:51,481 --> 00:14:54,680
This low-cost adhesive is used in manufacturing boards made

276
00:14:54,681 --> 00:14:59,480
from wood particles, as well as in plywood.

277
00:14:59,481 --> 00:15:05,000
Throughout history, humans have used glues in making shelters.

278
00:15:05,001 --> 00:15:09,640
2,500 years ago, Nebuchadnezzar used natural tar, a petroleum

279
00:15:09,641 --> 00:15:14,040
resin in the construction of buildings in Babylon.

280
00:15:14,041 --> 00:15:17,240
And the Aztecs used a cement and blood adhesive mixture

281
00:15:17,340 --> 00:15:19,440
that's still holding the remains of their structures

282
00:15:19,540 --> 00:15:20,240
together.

283
00:15:22,940 --> 00:15:24,880
Today, in our modern homes and offices,

284
00:15:24,980 --> 00:15:28,380
we're surrounded by a variety of specialty glues that are often

285
00:15:28,480 --> 00:15:30,800
stronger than the materials they're holding together.

286
00:15:33,900 --> 00:15:37,400
A simple test of a wood glue bond, in this case, Elmer's

287
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:41,640
glue, demonstrates the strength of the bond versus the substrate,

288
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:42,640
the wood.

289
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:53,200
Good wood glue should have not only good strength, and in this case you have around 3,500 pounds

290
00:15:53,201 --> 00:15:57,180
per square inch, but a good wood glue should also have what we call substrate failure.

291
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:03,140
In this case, the wood was actually pulled apart, which means the glue was actually stronger

292
00:16:03,141 --> 00:16:05,480
than the wood itself. All right, so what I'm going to do

293
00:16:05,481 --> 00:16:11,540
now is a dowel joint. In a dowel joint, small wood fittings

294
00:16:11,541 --> 00:16:15,180
or dowels are glued into drilled holes.

295
00:16:15,181 --> 00:16:17,680
With the help of wood glue, they hold the joint together

296
00:16:17,681 --> 00:16:18,980
without nails or screws.

297
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:26,520
These will be used for picture frame, door frame, furniture,

298
00:16:26,521 --> 00:16:28,680
anywhere you don't want to see an exterior fastener

299
00:16:28,681 --> 00:16:30,360
where it's going to have to get patched.

300
00:16:30,361 --> 00:16:31,200
So it's great.

301
00:16:31,201 --> 00:16:32,200
It's strong.

302
00:16:32,201 --> 00:16:33,560
The holes have been drilled.

303
00:16:33,561 --> 00:16:35,940
We will now apply the glue.

304
00:16:35,941 --> 00:16:39,540
Just a little dab will do you.

305
00:16:39,541 --> 00:16:45,280
Tap it in, put it together, clamp it tight, clean up the

306
00:16:45,380 --> 00:16:47,280
glue, set it aside.

307
00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:49,280
That's it.

308
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:52,280
Not all woods are created equal.

309
00:16:52,380 --> 00:16:55,180
Rubber woods in the Far East are denser and oilier than

310
00:16:55,280 --> 00:16:58,000
maples and oaks.

311
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:00,600
These woods are used to make furniture, plywood, and

312
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:02,300
parquet flooring.

313
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:05,200
And the adhesives that we use in this country just do not

314
00:17:05,300 --> 00:17:08,900
work properly on those woods.

315
00:17:08,901 --> 00:17:16,800
So a new line of products had to be developed to satisfy the woodworking industry, a lot of which

316
00:17:16,801 --> 00:17:22,780
it moved to the Far East. Another construction process,

317
00:17:23,080 --> 00:17:26,880
painting, relies on a different type of adhesive, masking tape.

318
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:29,800
Painters use tapes to paint within the lines.

319
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:34,400
This hardware store staple was the brainchild of 3M inventor Dick Drew,

320
00:17:34,401 --> 00:17:36,140
who noticed that automotive painters

321
00:17:36,240 --> 00:17:37,800
were having trouble making sharp lines

322
00:17:37,900 --> 00:17:40,740
on the two-toned cars that had become popular in the 1920s.

323
00:17:44,540 --> 00:17:48,140
Drew developed a product that created an impermanent bond,

324
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:51,660
a pressure-sensitive masking tape.

325
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:54,160
And he was able to come up with a product that

326
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:56,860
was permanently and aggressively sticky,

327
00:17:56,960 --> 00:18:00,660
but removable at the same time.

328
00:18:00,760 --> 00:18:04,400
Pressure-sensitive adhesives have a split personality.

329
00:18:04,401 --> 00:18:08,140
You have to imagine the material neither being a liquid

330
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:09,640
nor a solid.

331
00:18:09,740 --> 00:18:11,980
It is something else in between.

332
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:15,940
And we call that material a viscoelastic material.

333
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:18,720
That means it has viscous properties like a liquid,

334
00:18:18,820 --> 00:18:23,260
but it has elastic properties like a solid.

335
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:26,360
Tapes soon found other applications.

336
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:30,000
In the 1930s, Drew went on to coat samples of cellophane

337
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:32,960
with 3M adhesive to invent Scotch tape.

338
00:18:32,961 --> 00:18:36,300
Dick Drew made notice of the fact that people were starting

339
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:39,700
to use cellophane as a means of packaging foodstuffs.

340
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:42,300
And he thought, wouldn't it be wonderful to have a cellophane

341
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:45,400
back tape, which one could apply to that cellophane,

342
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:50,420
have it stick, and of course be openable later on.

343
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:53,580
3M, Drew's employer, was founded in 1902

344
00:18:53,680 --> 00:18:55,120
to produce sandpaper products.

345
00:18:58,060 --> 00:19:01,220
Today, it offers a full line of adhesives designed for everything

346
00:19:01,221 --> 00:19:06,020
from seats to signs, to versatile Post-It notes, known for their

347
00:19:06,021 --> 00:19:12,340
ability to be stuck, unstuck, and restuck.

348
00:19:12,341 --> 00:19:15,540
A repositionable glue like the Post-It notes is a far cry in

349
00:19:15,541 --> 00:19:18,580
terms of strength from this construction adhesive developed

350
00:19:18,581 --> 00:19:22,500
at Henkel. A reactive two-component room

351
00:19:22,501 --> 00:19:26,020
temperature curing adhesive system, this glue is especially

352
00:19:26,021 --> 00:19:30,980
designed for galvanized surfaces. When subjected to

353
00:19:30,981 --> 00:19:33,740
to thousands of pounds of pressure per square inch,

354
00:19:33,741 --> 00:19:37,160
the steel will tear before the glue fails.

355
00:19:37,161 --> 00:19:40,520
This is 16 gauge steel, okay?

356
00:19:40,521 --> 00:19:42,980
And look what we've done to the 16 gauge steel.

357
00:19:42,981 --> 00:19:45,060
The adhesive is completely intact.

358
00:19:45,061 --> 00:19:46,340
Look what we've done to the steel.

359
00:19:46,341 --> 00:19:48,380
We've actually ripped the steel.

360
00:19:48,381 --> 00:19:52,100
And this happens at about 5,200 to 6,000 pounds.

361
00:19:53,440 --> 00:19:56,860
One of the major deterrents to using light gauge steel

362
00:19:56,861 --> 00:20:03,040
in construction today is the cost of making the connections.

363
00:20:03,041 --> 00:20:05,900
In order to get the proper load distribution,

364
00:20:05,901 --> 00:20:09,940
you have to use a lot of screws.

365
00:20:09,941 --> 00:20:12,040
Using our adhesive system, you're

366
00:20:12,041 --> 00:20:18,940
talking about saving 80% to 90% of the labor cost.

367
00:20:18,941 --> 00:20:22,860
At ITW TAC, labor savings is just one of the considerations

368
00:20:22,861 --> 00:20:25,180
for its adhesive systems.

369
00:20:25,181 --> 00:20:29,960
Subfloors are made up of plywood bonded to the joists.

370
00:20:29,961 --> 00:20:34,000
When you put the bead of subfloor adhesive on the joists,

371
00:20:34,001 --> 00:20:36,880
drop the plywood in place, and then screw it down,

372
00:20:36,881 --> 00:20:39,760
what that does is it fills any of the voids.

373
00:20:39,761 --> 00:20:42,800
And it also has a little bit of give to it.

374
00:20:42,801 --> 00:20:46,500
When the wood is expanding and contracting with the seasons,

375
00:20:46,501 --> 00:20:50,000
it's going to help that not squeak.

376
00:20:50,001 --> 00:20:53,460
Subfloor and drywall adhesives, they're primarily

377
00:20:53,461 --> 00:20:57,840
up of a synthetic rubber polymer, some resin, some

378
00:20:57,940 --> 00:21:00,860
antioxidants, and a lot of filler.

379
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:03,300
And the filler is what gives it the body.

380
00:21:03,400 --> 00:21:06,300
And when you extrude a bead to put on the substrate,

381
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:08,440
it just sits there until you make the bond.

382
00:21:10,980 --> 00:21:13,280
Overhead, a ceiling can be held up

383
00:21:13,380 --> 00:21:16,480
by a two-part polyurethane foam without the help of screws

384
00:21:16,580 --> 00:21:19,480
and nails.

385
00:21:19,580 --> 00:21:22,760
As the adhesive is applied, it mixes and reacts,

386
00:21:22,761 --> 00:21:26,200
foaming to fill seams and hardening as it cures.

387
00:21:26,300 --> 00:21:28,200
This is a revolutionary application

388
00:21:28,300 --> 00:21:30,760
that was developed for manufactured housing

389
00:21:30,860 --> 00:21:32,400
in the late 1960s.

390
00:21:32,500 --> 00:21:34,800
It allowed manufactured home builders

391
00:21:34,900 --> 00:21:37,100
to create a finished ceiling panel

392
00:21:37,200 --> 00:21:39,420
without the use of any mechanical fasteners.

393
00:21:42,320 --> 00:21:43,920
Another fast-acting adhesive

394
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:47,420
is found inside countertops.

395
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:49,760
Countertops are typically put together

396
00:21:49,860 --> 00:21:51,520
with a contact adhesive.

397
00:21:51,521 --> 00:21:55,160
That's where the wood substrate, the particle board typically,

398
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:58,720
and the high-pressure laminate each get a coating of adhesive.

399
00:21:58,820 --> 00:22:02,400
When that film is dry in two to five minutes,

400
00:22:02,500 --> 00:22:05,860
the bond is made, and it's an instantaneous bond.

401
00:22:09,880 --> 00:22:11,780
The challenges a contact adhesive

402
00:22:11,880 --> 00:22:15,580
will see on a day-to-day basis in a home

403
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:20,820
is it might be bonding the laminate down right next to a sink.

404
00:22:20,821 --> 00:22:23,320
And I know at my house, the kids spill a lot of water

405
00:22:23,420 --> 00:22:25,360
from the sink onto the countertop,

406
00:22:25,460 --> 00:22:27,660
so it has to have water resistance,

407
00:22:27,760 --> 00:22:30,060
as well as right next to the stove.

408
00:22:30,160 --> 00:22:33,060
If the stove's on high and that countertop

409
00:22:33,160 --> 00:22:35,160
comes a couple of inches away, it's

410
00:22:35,260 --> 00:22:37,060
got to be able to withstand those temperatures.

411
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:43,580
At home, you're surrounded by glue.

412
00:22:43,680 --> 00:22:46,480
But if you think you can get away from it by leaving,

413
00:22:46,580 --> 00:22:48,020
think again.

414
00:22:48,021 --> 00:22:56,020
Whether by land or by sea, glue is on the move.

415
00:22:56,120 --> 00:23:00,460
Each year, over 40 billion pressure-sensitive self-adhesive

416
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:04,260
stamps are used in the United States.

417
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:06,980
Glue will return on Modern Marvels.

418
00:23:13,080 --> 00:23:15,680
We now return to Glue on Modern Marvels.

419
00:23:18,020 --> 00:23:25,000
Whether you're dry or all wet, if you're going, you're going

420
00:23:25,001 --> 00:23:26,700
with glue.

421
00:23:26,701 --> 00:23:29,140
The trend in the automotive industry is typical of the

422
00:23:29,141 --> 00:23:33,140
transportation sector at large.

423
00:23:33,141 --> 00:23:36,580
Advances in the materials cars are made of have transformed the

424
00:23:36,581 --> 00:23:39,200
way they're held together.

425
00:23:39,201 --> 00:23:42,720
The first cars were made from one material, steel, and held

426
00:23:42,721 --> 00:23:46,440
together with welds, bolts, and rivets.

427
00:23:46,441 --> 00:23:48,940
That's no longer the case.

428
00:23:49,040 --> 00:23:51,640
Your car's composition, which used to be predominantly,

429
00:23:51,740 --> 00:23:55,180
you know, steel, has moved a lot towards things like aluminum

430
00:23:55,280 --> 00:23:58,620
and plastics and urethanes and synthetics as well.

431
00:23:58,720 --> 00:24:03,020
And that evolution, driven by weight reduction in many cases,

432
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:06,260
as well as cost, has really opened up the ability for

433
00:24:06,360 --> 00:24:11,440
adhesives to be used in the assembly of cars.

434
00:24:11,540 --> 00:24:13,360
Adhesives provide excellent bonds

435
00:24:13,460 --> 00:24:15,560
between dissimilar materials.

436
00:24:15,561 --> 00:24:18,600
and seal cracks, and don't fracture more brittle

437
00:24:18,660 --> 00:24:23,360
composites, as a mechanical fastener might.

438
00:24:23,440 --> 00:24:26,200
One of the key adhesive applications in a car is around

439
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:28,280
the windshield. The front glass and the back

440
00:24:28,340 --> 00:24:31,240
glass are both urethaned in. So that's where part of the

441
00:24:31,300 --> 00:24:33,180
structural support, because as you can tell nowadays, the

442
00:24:33,240 --> 00:24:35,420
metal's nowhere near as thick as it used to be.

443
00:24:35,480 --> 00:24:41,560
So a lot relies on the glass holding the roof up in a rollover.

444
00:24:41,620 --> 00:24:43,180
During a windshield replacement,

445
00:24:43,181 --> 00:24:46,080
you can see how strong that urethane bond is when you try

446
00:24:46,180 --> 00:24:49,280
to remove it. This will cut that glue that's

447
00:24:49,380 --> 00:24:51,860
in here, which is usually about a half inch, maybe 3

448
00:24:51,960 --> 00:24:56,700
quarters of an inch wide. Once the broken windshield's

449
00:24:56,800 --> 00:25:01,660
out, it's time to apply the glue, 175 degree urethane, which

450
00:25:01,760 --> 00:25:04,560
cools and cures quickly. We've got about 8 minutes

451
00:25:04,660 --> 00:25:06,800
to get the windshield in. We want to get in there as

452
00:25:06,801 --> 00:25:07,800
as quick as we can.

453
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:15,100
So it'll be about an hour before they could drive away safely.

454
00:25:19,840 --> 00:25:22,480
When the adhesive is thought about in the front end

455
00:25:22,481 --> 00:25:27,020
of this design, it's providing lighter weight, less parts,

456
00:25:27,021 --> 00:25:29,400
ultimately less cost.

457
00:25:29,401 --> 00:25:32,460
There's a lot of labor involved with drilling a hole,

458
00:25:32,461 --> 00:25:35,100
placing the rivet, and making that component

459
00:25:35,101 --> 00:25:36,100
adhere to one another.

460
00:25:36,800 --> 00:25:39,440
A key breakthrough to replacing mechanical fasteners

461
00:25:39,540 --> 00:25:42,000
occurred in 1953.

462
00:25:42,100 --> 00:25:45,100
Chemist Dr. Vernon Kreeble of Hartford, Connecticut,

463
00:25:45,200 --> 00:25:47,640
solved the instability problem of a new adhesive

464
00:25:47,740 --> 00:25:50,540
that his chemist's son, Robert, had brought him.

465
00:25:50,640 --> 00:25:53,860
Vernon Kreeble developed a cure inhibition system

466
00:25:53,960 --> 00:25:57,560
for a unique liquid bonding resin that hardened anaerobically,

467
00:25:57,660 --> 00:25:59,360
or in the absence of air.

468
00:25:59,460 --> 00:26:01,760
As long as it's in contact with air,

469
00:26:01,860 --> 00:26:03,360
it remains a liquid.

470
00:26:03,460 --> 00:26:06,160
You put it onto a nut and bolt, or onto a bearing,

471
00:26:06,161 --> 00:26:07,660
or any shaft-mounted part.

472
00:26:07,760 --> 00:26:11,760
And what it does is it wicks its way between the threads.

473
00:26:11,860 --> 00:26:14,300
As soon as it's in the contact of metal

474
00:26:14,400 --> 00:26:16,700
and there's no oxygen for it to remain stable,

475
00:26:16,800 --> 00:26:18,400
it hardens to essentially a tough plastic.

476
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:24,720
The product, Loctite, solved the problem

477
00:26:24,820 --> 00:26:28,220
of loose nuts and bolts in machines and appliances,

478
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:30,660
and is still the cornerstone of the company's adhesives

479
00:26:30,760 --> 00:26:33,660
business.

480
00:26:33,760 --> 00:26:35,920
Loctite products can be found in many of the cars

481
00:26:35,921 --> 00:26:36,621
on the road.

482
00:26:41,660 --> 00:26:43,820
But the adhesive's usefulness is most dramatic

483
00:26:43,920 --> 00:26:47,660
in high-performance vehicles, where vibration is intense,

484
00:26:47,760 --> 00:26:50,560
and you can't afford to have a screw loose.

485
00:26:50,660 --> 00:26:54,880
The Daytona 500, virtually every one of those cars

486
00:26:54,980 --> 00:26:56,080
is held together.

487
00:26:56,180 --> 00:26:58,380
The threaded parts especially are held together

488
00:26:58,480 --> 00:27:00,080
with Loctite thread lockers.

489
00:27:00,180 --> 00:27:03,680
In the 60s, part of racing was cleaning the track up,

490
00:27:03,681 --> 00:27:11,440
and they would literally go around the track and with a sweeper and clean up nuts and bolts that may have come off during the race.

491
00:27:12,220 --> 00:27:20,420
If you look at Formula One or NASCAR, these adhesives widely used are gasketing silicones, thread lockers, retaining compounds.

492
00:27:20,800 --> 00:27:28,560
These, again, are being used to be able to provide a quality race car that's not going to have loose fasteners,

493
00:27:28,660 --> 00:27:32,060
that's not going to have gaskets that are going to leak mid-race.

494
00:27:33,680 --> 00:27:37,680
Similar considerations are just as crucial on streets and

495
00:27:37,681 --> 00:27:41,680
highways. During any given rush hour, there

496
00:27:41,681 --> 00:27:45,680
are literally tons of adhesives on the road.

497
00:27:45,681 --> 00:27:48,680
A car's door panels held in with an adhesive.

498
00:27:48,681 --> 00:27:51,680
The seats foam laminated to their coverings.

499
00:27:51,681 --> 00:27:54,680
The dashboard components, multiple layers with adhesive

500
00:27:54,681 --> 00:27:57,680
laminates. The headliner, a vacuum formed

501
00:27:57,681 --> 00:28:00,680
laminated material. The rear view mirrors held on with

502
00:28:00,681 --> 00:28:03,280
adhesive. Air filters, rubber trim,

503
00:28:03,281 --> 00:28:06,820
and gasketing contain glues. And of course, there are adhesives

504
00:28:06,821 --> 00:28:10,280
in the computers that increasingly run the automobile.

505
00:28:10,281 --> 00:28:13,220
Headlights and taillights, adhesively sealed to keep water

506
00:28:13,221 --> 00:28:16,060
out. Pin striping and side molding,

507
00:28:16,061 --> 00:28:19,360
held on with high performance pressure-sensitive adhesives.

508
00:28:19,361 --> 00:28:23,360
